//! Fixture for SL-316 — a CLOSURE BODY that really parks, written in an
//! IMPORTED module.
//!
//! The three helpers below are the cell SL-316 found: a closure literal handed
//! to a conservative-only std method (`Vector.each`, whose only suspension cause
//! is the rule that a call through a non-`sync` function value might), with a
//! real cooperative primitive inside it. The enclosing function therefore
//! carries NO cause of its own, no frame is built for it, no frame builder ever
//! scans its body — and before design 275 U2 the `yield_now()` lowered outside
//! every frame, where it codegens to nothing. The program compiled and printed
//! `0` drive steps where two were owed, while the byte-identical entry-module
//! twin was refused.
//!
//! They live in a module of their OWN because the refusal is now a property of
//! the closure rather than of who calls it: any test importing this module is
//! refused, which is exactly the point, and is why these three could not stay in
//! `sl306mod` beside helpers four passing tests import.

import std.task.{yield_now}

/// `public`, reached by a selective import.
public func each_yield_public(values: &Vector<Int>) -> Int {
    values.each({ n in
        yield_now()
    })
    values.len()
}

/// MODULE-PRIVATE — the one design 249 tags with a `$m$<module>` symbol, which
/// is the keying SL-280 found the splice reading two ways.
func _each_yield_private(values: &Vector<Int>) -> Int {
    values.each({ n in
        yield_now()
    })
    values.len()
}

/// The same reached one hop further in, through the private helper.
public func each_yield_via_private(values: &Vector<Int>) -> Int {
    _each_yield_private(&values)
}
